WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.000
Today curator Ben Russell is going to show me

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.000
something that Victorians might have

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:08.280
got up to in their equivalent of your
garden shed. In another

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:13.600
These look incredible and they look 3D
printed to me but I'm guessing they

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:15.219
are a little bit older than that?

00:00:15.219 --> 00:00:16.940
A bit before 3D printing.

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:19.859
If you're a mechanically minded chap or woman actually,

00:00:19.859 --> 00:00:23.100
and you wanted to play with
machines, before the car came along,

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:25.220
you'd have a lathe. A lathe like this.

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:27.083
This is just the bare bones of the machine.

00:00:27.083 --> 00:00:28.860
You would have a chuck here which holds

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:31.890
a piece of material like Ivory or Boxwood or something like that.

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:35.430
And then as you rotated it here you've
got a treadle here, so

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:38.240
like a Singer sewing machine you would treadle away and that would turn the piece.

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:41.100
And you would have a cutting tool here which would cut into it.

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.980
and what they're doing is reproducing remarkable decorative pieces like this.

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:45.940
If you pick this up, you think that looks a little bit strange,

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:49.480
but then you turn it through 90 degrees and realize it's the Duke of Wellington, which is really quite smashing.

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:53.100
So is this all carved from one continuous piece? Actually what is it?

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:54.510
Not always.

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:58.410
This would be fossil ivory - no
issue there - because fossil Ivory would

00:00:58.410 --> 00:01:01.730
have been lying around on the floor for millennia from dead elephants which had died.

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:05.800
And so basically you would be producing
little tiny parts which would

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.720
have gradually slot together; some
of the parts are quite big and then you

00:01:08.729 --> 00:01:11.200
get some of these little tiny decorative
bits like these.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:16.250
So the idea is you would  buy the lathe and you would buy the attachments. Ah, we have the manual.

00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:21.340
Absolutely so here we have Holtzapffel's Turning and Mechanical Manipulation Volume 5.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:23.820
There's a sixth volume in which
they never planned.

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:28.680
And what you have here... this is your guidebook to your world of mechanical wonders

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:32.040
in ornamental turning.
And as you can see once you start opening,

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:35.490
you have these worked examples which were produced to say look you can do this too.

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:38.939
So is this the sort of thing
that someone would have in their shed?

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:44.480
Yes in a way this is the proto shed;  some
place you would go and hide away from your family

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:47.880
and have a smoke and probably some alcohol and then do that sort of thing.

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:50.549
So people with these would have
a workshop and you go away and you

00:01:50.549 --> 00:01:53.640
produce turning and very often these
pieces were designed to be

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:55.800
shown off. You're showing your skill as a turner

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:56.560
by producing a piece like this.

00:01:56.900 --> 00:02:00.760
And you would display it in your drawing room or living room.

